<speak>
<p>Write h as the sum of h sub j times X to the j. Its star is the sum of h sub k times X to the negative k. Multiply these sums: each pair contributes h sub j times h sub k, at exponent j minus k. Exponents are read modulo n.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>In a four-position illustration, arrange the sixteen pairwise products in a grid. Each entry belongs to the coefficient indexed by j minus k modulo four. To collect a single coefficient, select exactly those cells with the same exponent.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Fix an exponent a. A term contributes to X to the a precisely when j minus k is congruent to a modulo n. Equivalently, k is j minus a modulo n. Thus the coefficient of X to the a in h times h star is the sum, over j, of h sub j times h sub j minus a.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>But h sub j minus a is the jth entry of the row shifted a places right. Our coefficient is therefore the dot product of the original row with that shifted row. The coefficient notation always refers to the unique representative of degree less than n.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Check the original row, negative one, one, one, one. Shift zero gives dot product four. Shifts one, two, and three each give zero: two negative contributions cancel two positive contributions. So the product has coefficients four, zero, zero, zero, and equals the constant polynomial four.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For general n, collect every exponent in the same way. The product h times h star is the sum over a of the cyclic correlation at shift a, times X to the a. This identity works for arbitrary integer coefficients. For sign coefficients, the constant correlation is n. That extra hypothesis enters in the Hadamard dictionary.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
